    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-hashi">Sho Hashi</a> unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-island">Okinawa</a> in 1429 and ruled from Shuri as a tributary king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming China</a>.</li><li>Royal ships carried Chinese, Japanese and Southeast Asian goods, making Ryukyu a major trading hub in the 15th and 16th centuries.</li><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>'s invasion in 1609 it served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> at once, until Meiji Japan annexed it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-prefecture">Okinawa Prefecture</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The bronze bell cast for Shuri Castle in 1458 bears an inscription calling Ryukyu a 'bridge of all nations', linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> by its ships.</p></aside>
    </section>

